244
00:14:13,316 --> 00:14:17,435
model years, multiple hardware
generations, and hardware abstraction

245
00:14:17,805 --> 00:14:19,075
create the foundation for reuse.

246
00:14:19,075 --> 00:14:26,866
And reu-reuse is one of the keys of
scalability because if every new platform

247
00:14:26,866 --> 00:14:32,445
requires rebuilding validation assets
from scratch, no amount of automation

248
00:14:32,445 --> 00:14:35,545
can save us Now coming to the next slide.

249
00:14:35,545 --> 00:14:38,645
The next two layers works very
closely together, so I'll be

250
00:14:38,645 --> 00:14:39,965
discussing them as a pair.

251
00:14:40,735 --> 00:14:42,475
Let's start with the real-time simulation.

252
00:14:42,535 --> 00:14:46,505
One of the greatest strengths of
HIL testing has always been with the

253
00:14:46,555 --> 00:14:50,735
ability to create scenarios that are
difficult to reproduce in a physical way.

254
00:14:51,415 --> 00:14:53,835
Think about some problems
we all encountered.

255
00:14:53,915 --> 00:14:57,965
An intermittent communication issue,
a sensor fault that only appears

256
00:14:58,095 --> 00:15:02,825
occasionally, a startup rare condition,
a low voltage condition combined with

257
00:15:02,825 --> 00:15:04,355
the heavy network traffic almost.

258
00:15:04,875 --> 00:15:09,055
These issues can extremely be
difficult to recreate consistently.

259
00:15:09,515 --> 00:15:12,765
Sometimes you spend more time
reproducing the problem than fixing it.

260
00:15:13,285 --> 00:15:14,405
Simulation changes that.

261
00:15:14,645 --> 00:15:17,715
Simulation allows us to create
deterministic conditions.

262
00:15:17,765 --> 00:15:21,635
If I run the same scenario ten times,
I want the same condition ten times.

263
00:15:22,385 --> 00:15:25,705
That consistency becomes
incredibly valuable when debugging

264
00:15:25,735 --> 00:15:27,545
complex software interactions.

265
00:15:29,295 --> 00:15:30,335
Let me give you an example.

266
00:15:31,545 --> 00:15:36,075
Imagine a customer reports a feature
occasionally fails during vehicle startup.

267
00:15:36,075 --> 00:15:40,655
Maybe happens only once or twice
after low ba-battery condition

268
00:15:40,705 --> 00:15:42,745
combined with a heavy network load.

269
00:15:43,395 --> 00:15:47,425
Replicating that situation in a physical
vehicle could take hours together.

270
00:15:47,955 --> 00:15:52,175
With simulations, we can recreate
that exact condition repeatedly until

271
00:15:52,195 --> 00:15:53,285
we understand what is happening.

272
00:15:53,445 --> 00:15:55,245
Now let's talk about signal governance.

273
00:15:55,845 --> 00:15:58,855
This may sound less exciting
than simulation, but honestly,

274
00:15:59,535 --> 00:16:00,885
it solves a lot of problems.

275
00:16:01,695 --> 00:16:05,065
I've seen situations with three
different teams use three different

276
00:16:05,075 --> 00:16:06,945
names for the same signal.

277
00:16:07,915 --> 00:16:11,315
One team called it vehicle speed,
one team… another team called

278
00:16:12,285 --> 00:16:13,635
vehicle short form with speed.

279
00:16:13,885 --> 00:16:15,725
Another team called it speed value.

280
00:16:16,915 --> 00:16:20,055
Everyone believed they are
referring the same information.

281
00:16:21,255 --> 00:16:23,675
Eventually, interaction
issues starts appearing.

282
00:16:24,265 --> 00:16:27,775
Team spends time arguing over data
when they should be solving problems.

283
00:16:28,585 --> 00:16:31,125
Signal governance creates
signal source of truth.

284
00:16:31,865 --> 00:16:34,945
Every signal has a standardized
definition, a standard name, a

285
00:16:35,315 --> 00:16:39,445
standard scaling factor, a standard
ownership model, a version control.

286
00:16:39,445 --> 00:16:42,255
The result is consistent
and the organization.

287
00:16:43,615 --> 00:16:49,035
And consistency is one of the foundation
of observability, because before we

288
00:16:49,035 --> 00:16:53,595
can observe a system effectively,
we first need confidence that

289
00:16:53,715 --> 00:16:55,895
everyone is observing the same thing

290
00:16:56,895 --> 00:16:57,915
Now coming to this slide.

291
00:16:57,955 --> 00:17:01,115
If there's one slide I'd like
everyone to remember from this

292
00:17:01,115 --> 00:17:02,765
presentation, it's probably this one.

293
00:17:03,265 --> 00:17:07,785
When people hear the word observability,
they often think about logs, dashboards,

294
00:17:07,905 --> 00:17:09,295
metrics, or monitoring tools.

295
00:17:10,085 --> 00:17:15,035
Those things are important, but what
I'm talking about goes deeper than that.

296
00:17:15,435 --> 00:17:20,355
I'm talking about making diagnostics
a core architectural component

297
00:17:20,415 --> 00:17:21,685
of the validation framework.

298
00:17:22,945 --> 00:17:26,135
In many traditional environments,
diagnostics are something we

299
00:17:26,135 --> 00:17:28,155
look at after a test completes.

300
00:17:28,645 --> 00:17:32,285
We run the test, something fails,
then we start gathering information.

301
00:17:32,285 --> 00:17:38,415
We collect logs, we run the scenarios, we
capture traces, we start investigating.

302
00:17:38,415 --> 00:17:39,825
The process can take a lot of time.

303
00:17:40,146 --> 00:17:43,925
Instead, imagine diagnostics being
built directly into the framework.

304
00:17:44,795 --> 00:17:47,145
Every execution automatically
generates telemetry.

305
00:17:48,285 --> 00:17:50,015
Every single transition is recorded.

306
00:17:50,545 --> 00:17:52,335
Diagnostic double quotes are captured.

307
00:17:52,735 --> 00:17:54,275
Timing relationships are tracked.

308
00:17:54,705 --> 00:17:56,225
Network interactions are monitored.

309
00:17:56,725 --> 00:17:58,845
Service-to-service
communication becomes visible.

310
00:18:00,125 --> 00:18:03,135
Now, let me give you an
ex-- practical example.

311
00:18:03,225 --> 00:18:05,145
Suppose you're validating a large system.

312
00:18:05,205 --> 00:18:08,835
Most test execution completes
successfully, but occasionally you

313
00:18:08,835 --> 00:18:10,406
see an intermittent DTC appear.

314
00:18:10,985 --> 00:18:13,755
These are exactly the kind of issues
that consume engineering time.

315
00:18:15,065 --> 00:18:18,175
In traditional setup, you may discover
the DTC after the test completes,

316
00:18:18,175 --> 00:18:19,956
then the investigation begins.

317
00:18:19,956 --> 00:18:24,165
With embedded diagnostics, the
framework already has the context.

318
00:18:24,245 --> 00:18:27,095
It already knows what
message was sent, exchanged.

319
00:18:28,175 --> 00:18:30,395
It already knows what
signals were changed.

320
00:18:30,775 --> 00:18:32,715
It already knows what
services were active.

321
00:18:33,775 --> 00:18:36,765
It already knows the precise
timing sequencing that occurred

322
00:18:36,765 --> 00:18:38,005
before the DTC appeared.

323
00:18:38,595 --> 00:18:41,025
That's a big, huge advantage.

324
00:18:41,085 --> 00:18:45,005
Instead of asking what happened, you
immediately start asking, "When did

325
00:18:45,005 --> 00:18:49,285
it happen?" That shift dramatically
accelerates root cause analysis.

326
00:18:49,765 --> 00:18:54,126
And as the software system continue
becoming more inter-interconnected, I

327
00:18:54,126 --> 00:18:58,265
believe this level of visibility becomes
increasingly important because modern

328
00:18:58,265 --> 00:19:02,505
failures rarely occurs instead inside
one isolated component, they occur

329
00:19:02,865 --> 00:19:04,666
at the boundaries behind the system.

330
00:19:05,966 --> 00:19:10,896
Observability helps us… these
boundaries clear Now coming

331
00:19:10,896 --> 00:19:12,626
to the automation orchestra.

332
00:19:12,736 --> 00:19:14,926
Let now let's talk about automation.

333
00:19:15,486 --> 00:19:18,326
Most organization already
use automation in some forms.

334
00:19:19,236 --> 00:19:23,006
But what's important here isn't simply
automating tests, it's automating

335
00:19:23,016 --> 00:19:24,736
the entire validation workflow.

336
00:19:25,536 --> 00:19:28,226
One challenge with manual
testing variability.

337
00:19:29,246 --> 00:19:33,076
Even experienced engineers execute
procedures slightly different.

338
00:19:33,766 --> 00:19:36,506
One person wait five seconds
before triggering any event,

339
00:19:36,846 --> 00:19:38,006
another might wait six seconds.

340
00:19:38,436 --> 00:19:40,776
Another performs the steps in
slightly different sequences.

341
00:19:41,426 --> 00:19:42,786
Usually, the difference are minor.

342
00:19:43,826 --> 00:19:47,426
But as system becomes more time
sensitive, small variations

343
00:19:47,426 --> 00:19:49,546
can influence the results.

344
00:19:49,546 --> 00:19:51,326
Let me give a small example here.

345
00:19:51,376 --> 00:19:55,036
Imagine a three engineers running the
same power liftgate validation scenario.

346
00:19:55,996 --> 00:19:57,746
The test procedure is identical.

347
00:19:58,266 --> 00:20:02,456
However, the timing between
the steps varies slightly.

348
00:20:02,456 --> 00:20:05,536
Now, suppose a feature has an
intermittent timing-related defect.

349
00:20:05,976 --> 00:20:08,206
One engineer sees the
issue, another doesn't.

350
00:20:08,586 --> 00:20:10,786
Now everyone is debating whether
the issue is real or not.

351
00:20:11,536 --> 00:20:14,076
You're probably all being in
the same situation like that.

352
00:20:15,625 --> 00:20:18,625
Automated orchestra
eliminates the uncertainty.

353
00:20:19,106 --> 00:20:20,916
The framework control execution.

354
00:20:21,265 --> 00:20:22,706
The framework controls the timings.

355
00:20:23,136 --> 00:20:25,166
The framework controls
the diagnostic collection.

356
00:20:25,706 --> 00:20:27,886
The framework controls evaluation.

357
00:20:28,386 --> 00:20:30,066
Every run is executed consistently.

358
00:20:31,026 --> 00:20:33,096
Every result is reproducible.

359
00:20:33,875 --> 00:20:37,116
And because diagnostics are
integrated into the architecture,

360
00:20:37,146 --> 00:20:38,936
automation becomes more powerful.

361
00:20:40,085 --> 00:20:44,156
Instead of simply executing
tests, the framework can react

362
00:20:44,166 --> 00:20:45,905
to the observed behavior.

363
00:20:46,346 --> 00:20:49,486
If diagnostic condition appears,
the workflow automatically collects

364
00:20:49,496 --> 00:20:51,375
additional information it…

365
00:20:51,846 --> 00:20:56,436
If unexpected behavior occurs, additional
validation path can be triggered.

366
00:20:56,706 --> 00:21:00,396
The validation process becomes
closed loop rather than static.

367
00:21:00,896 --> 00:21:04,195
And that's important because
software-defined systems are becoming

368
00:21:04,316 --> 00:21:07,335
too complex for purely manual approaches.

369
00:21:08,335 --> 00:21:09,885
The goal isn't replacing engineers.

370
00:21:09,965 --> 00:21:14,295
The goal is freeing engineers from
repetitive execution tasks, so they can

371
00:21:14,325 --> 00:21:19,535
focus on understanding system behavior
and solving problems, and that's exactly

372
00:21:19,715 --> 00:21:22,265
what automation helps us achieve.

373
00:21:23,265 --> 00:21:26,525
Now coming to the slide, the
closed-loop workflow in practice.

374
00:21:27,055 --> 00:21:30,325
Now that we have talked about
automation, let's look at one of the

375
00:21:30,335 --> 00:21:34,255
most powerful outcomes it enables,
and that's closed-loop validation.

376
00:21:34,765 --> 00:21:37,855
Traditionally, validation has
been fairly linear process.

377
00:21:38,165 --> 00:21:42,035
We execute a test case, we collect
results, we review logs, we analyze

378
00:21:42,215 --> 00:21:44,615
failures, then we decide what to do next.

379
00:21:45,005 --> 00:21:49,025
In other words, the test itself
doesn't really react to what it's

380
00:21:49,025 --> 00:21:50,395
observing, the engineer does.

381
00:21:51,605 --> 00:21:54,265
And what if the validation
framework could participate in

382
00:21:54,275 --> 00:21:55,225
the decision-making process?

383
00:21:55,535 --> 00:21:57,685
And that's really the idea behind
the closed-loop validation.

384
00:21:58,585 --> 00:22:01,945
Instead of waiting until the end
of the test frame, the framework

385
00:22:01,985 --> 00:22:06,285
continuously monitors system behavior
while the scenario is executing.

386
00:22:07,305 --> 00:22:11,375
It's constantly asking question:
Are signal behaving as expected?

387
00:22:11,655 --> 00:22:13,105
Are timing requirements met?

388
00:22:13,575 --> 00:22:15,935
Are software services
reported consistently?

389
00:22:16,395 --> 00:22:17,975
Has diagnostics event occurred?

390
00:22:18,275 --> 00:22:20,055
Is a network message delayed or missing?

391
00:22:21,325 --> 00:22:24,705
Rather than treating the validation
as a scenario of disconnected test

392
00:22:24,775 --> 00:22:28,575
executions, we create a closed
loop, feedback loop sort of thing.

393
00:22:29,115 --> 00:22:30,715
Let me give you a practical example.

394
00:22:32,245 --> 00:22:35,735
Imagine a new software dropped in
is delivered overnight, probably.

395
00:22:35,815 --> 00:22:38,125
The next morning, the validation
environment automatically

396
00:22:38,375 --> 00:22:40,165
starts regression testing.

397
00:22:40,695 --> 00:22:44,545
During execution, the framework
notices that service response

398
00:22:44,545 --> 00:22:47,545
time is inconsistent, slower
than the baseline model.

399
00:22:48,395 --> 00:22:49,545
The feature still works, though.

400
00:22:50,155 --> 00:22:54,075
The test may technically pass,
but something has changed.

401
00:22:54,545 --> 00:22:58,085
In a traditional environment,
the subtle degradation might go

402
00:22:58,155 --> 00:22:59,895
unnoticed until a week later.

403
00:23:00,355 --> 00:23:05,145
In closed-loop environment, the framework
immediately detects that deviation.

404
00:23:05,925 --> 00:23:06,885
It flags the behavior.

405
00:23:07,595 --> 00:23:08,725
It stores telemetry.

406
00:23:08,965 --> 00:23:12,555
It may even trigger additional
investigation scenarios automatically.

407
00:23:13,585 --> 00:23:18,415
That incredibility value because many
issues become a small deviation before

408
00:23:18,415 --> 00:23:20,175
becoming a customer-facing defect.

409
00:23:21,085 --> 00:23:23,865
Another example might be
network communication.

410
00:23:24,495 --> 00:23:29,645
Suppose one service is expected to
publish a message every five hundred

411
00:23:29,645 --> 00:23:31,185
or probably hundred milliseconds.

412
00:23:31,345 --> 00:23:35,825
If the framework sees those intervals
increasing unexpectedly, it can

413
00:23:35,845 --> 00:23:40,145
immediately identify the issue rather than
waiting for a downstream failure to occur.

414
00:23:41,085 --> 00:23:44,825
What I really like about closed-loop
validation is that it shifts the mindset

415
00:23:44,825 --> 00:23:50,065
from simply verifying requirement to
continuously evaluating system health.

416
00:23:50,665 --> 00:23:53,945
We're not just asking, "Did the test
pass?" We are asking, "How did the

417
00:23:54,215 --> 00:23:59,015
system behave while it passed?" And
in software-defined vehicle, that's

418
00:23:59,115 --> 00:24:04,885
becoming just as important because quite
often the early warning signs for future

419
00:24:04,885 --> 00:24:06,715
failure are already visible in the data.

420
00:24:07,465 --> 00:24:09,875
We just need the framework
capable for recognizing them.

421
00:24:10,275 --> 00:24:13,425
That's exactly what
closed-loop validation enables.

422
00:24:14,425 --> 00:24:17,395
The next topic that I want
to discuss is scalability.

423
00:24:17,885 --> 00:24:21,195
Because we are honest, scalability
is one of the biggest challenges

424
00:24:21,215 --> 00:24:23,135
facing validation organizations today.

425
00:24:24,215 --> 00:24:26,225
Vehicle software continues growing.

426
00:24:26,665 --> 00:24:29,135
Feature complexity continues growing.

427
00:24:29,955 --> 00:24:33,175
The number of interactions
between system continues growing.

428
00:24:33,725 --> 00:24:37,095
But engineering resources don't
necessarily grow at the same rate.

429
00:24:37,105 --> 00:24:40,395
So the question becomes, how do
we validate more software without

430
00:24:40,865 --> 00:24:44,375
requiring dramatically more
people and dramatically more time?

431
00:24:45,265 --> 00:24:47,195
The answer is, in my opinion, is reuse.

432
00:24:48,005 --> 00:24:50,315
Let's think about how validation
is often performed today.

433
00:24:51,225 --> 00:24:55,665
A team developed that the test
assets for one vehicle, the program

434
00:24:56,105 --> 00:24:59,295
works perfectly fine, the work is
successful, the program launches.

435
00:24:59,895 --> 00:25:01,755
Then a second vehicle platform arrives.

436
00:25:01,845 --> 00:25:05,415
Even though many features are
similar, team often spends significant

437
00:25:05,435 --> 00:25:08,865
efforts rebuilding validation
content, new script, new mapping,

438
00:25:08,875 --> 00:25:10,265
new integration, new reporting.

439
00:25:10,765 --> 00:25:14,305
A lot of knowledge gets recreated.

440
00:25:14,895 --> 00:25:16,425
Now imagine a different approach.

441
00:25:16,495 --> 00:25:21,065
Instead of rebuilding those assets,
we treat them as a reusable component.

442
00:25:21,475 --> 00:25:25,735
Scenario libraries becomes reusable,
signal definitions become reusable,

443
00:25:26,315 --> 00:25:30,525
diagnostic probes becomes reusable,
automation workflow becomes reusable.

444
00:25:31,565 --> 00:25:33,615
The framework itself becomes reusable.

445
00:25:34,415 --> 00:25:35,783
Let me give you a simple example.

446
00:25:35,783 --> 00:25:40,495
Suppose we develop a validation
assets for a full-size SCV platform.

447
00:25:41,545 --> 00:25:46,105
A year later, pickup truck platform adopts
many of the same body control features.

448
00:25:46,205 --> 00:25:49,955
Without a scalable architecture,
engineer will spend months

449
00:25:49,995 --> 00:25:51,395
rebuilding the validation content.

450
00:25:52,055 --> 00:25:55,085
With the reusable architecture,
the majority of the validation

451
00:25:55,105 --> 00:25:56,295
logic already exist.

452
00:25:58,063 --> 00:26:02,513
The team only notes needs to adjust
to the configuration data rather than

453
00:26:02,553 --> 00:26:04,553
re-writing everything from scratch.

454
00:26:04,553 --> 00:26:06,573
That's the huge difference.

455
00:26:07,503 --> 00:26:10,503
The architecture shows here
support scalability in three ways.

456
00:26:10,503 --> 00:26:13,243
First, modular test assets.

457
00:26:14,163 --> 00:26:17,703
Tests are developed one
and reused many times.

458
00:26:17,703 --> 00:26:21,293
Second, configuration-driven deployment.

459
00:26:21,423 --> 00:26:25,023
Hardware-specific information
can be injected without

460
00:26:25,023 --> 00:26:26,763
changing core validation logic.

461
00:26:27,753 --> 00:26:29,363
Third, horizontally scalability.

462
00:26:30,573 --> 00:26:35,753
A new vehicle domains are introduced,
organization extends existing

463
00:26:35,753 --> 00:26:37,473
infrastructure than replacing it.

464
00:26:38,543 --> 00:26:42,353
The result is something where
every engineer organization wants.

465
00:26:42,893 --> 00:26:45,693
More validation coverage without
proportional increase in efforts.

466
00:26:46,753 --> 00:26:50,133
And that's ultimately
what scalability means.

467
00:26:50,133 --> 00:26:53,143
Not necessarily doing more
work, but getting more value

468
00:26:53,153 --> 00:26:54,213
from work we've already done.

469
00:26:55,213 --> 00:26:58,293
So when we come to this slide after
everything we have discussed so far,

470
00:26:58,313 --> 00:27:02,623
the natural question becomes: What do
we actually gain from this architecture?

471
00:27:03,273 --> 00:27:09,333
Why should organizations e-even invest
in observability-driven validation?

472
00:27:10,053 --> 00:27:10,830
Let's start with the signal integrity.

473
00:27:10,830 --> 00:27:13,143
When signals are governed
through centralized framework,

474
00:27:13,543 --> 00:27:17,023
inconsistencies become much
easier to identify and eliminate.

475
00:27:17,843 --> 00:27:20,913
Team stops arguing about signal
definition, then start focusing

476
00:27:20,933 --> 00:27:22,703
on solving actual problems.

477
00:27:23,473 --> 00:27:26,333
That alone can save surprising
amount of engineering time.

478
00:27:26,963 --> 00:27:30,573
The second outcome is
re-reduced defect escape rates.

479
00:27:30,853 --> 00:27:33,623
One thing I've learned throughout my
career is that earlier we discover

480
00:27:33,623 --> 00:27:35,643
a issue, the cheaper it is to fix.

481
00:27:36,773 --> 00:27:40,543
Embedded diagnostics and observability
help us identify problems much

482
00:27:40,613 --> 00:27:42,583
earlier development of li-life cycle.

483
00:27:43,493 --> 00:27:46,943
Instead of finding the issues
after integration or worse, after

484
00:27:46,943 --> 00:27:50,833
customer exposure, we detect them
during validation activities.

485
00:27:51,293 --> 00:27:54,073
That's a significant quality advantage.

486
00:27:54,593 --> 00:27:58,293
The third outcome is elimination
of operator variability.

487
00:27:58,293 --> 00:28:01,343
We've all seen situations where
different engineers execute similar

488
00:28:01,343 --> 00:28:03,903
procedures in different outcomes.

489
00:28:04,463 --> 00:28:06,463
Automation removes that inconsistencies.

490
00:28:06,833 --> 00:28:11,503
Execution becomes repeatable, results
